<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # How I Learned to Focus – Lesson # 4</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.1em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 4</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Writing</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Grade:</b> 10th Grade (CEFR: B1.1)</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Scenario:</b> Study Habits for Success</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Theme:</b> How I Learned to Focus</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Writing: Productive: Compose a detailed personal narrative. Interactive: Compile feedback for collaborative writing in a structured manner.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Writing: Can write a detailed narrative about a personal study experience, including reflections on what worked and what could be improved (e.g., "Last month, I realized I was falling behind in math. I started setting reminders to review my notes every day after dinner. Now, I feel more confident when solving equations."). Can contribute to a collaborative guide on study strategies by organizing peer feedback and adding recommendations (e.g., "Peers have suggested writing down homework in an agenda to avoid missing deadlines. Others have recommended setting phone reminders."). Can compile and paraphrase feedback on study habits during peer review sessions and organize it into a report (e.g., "The feedback suggests that making a study schedule and using color-coded notes improves time management.").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="background-color: #f2f2f2;">
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher displays a picture of a student looking frustrated at their books and another picture of a student smiling while studying effectively. Students are asked to briefly write down one thing they *used to do* that hindered their focus and one thing they *have done* to improve it. They then share their sentences with a partner. Example: "I used to procrastinate a lot, but now I have developed a consistent study routine."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models how to write a simple narrative sentence using the target grammar and vocabulary. For example, the teacher writes on the board: "I used to struggle with math assignments. Then, I set a goal to practice every day for 20 minutes. I have improved my grades significantly." The teacher stresses the pronunciation of "used to" and "have improved" (e.g., /juːst tuː/ and /hæv ɪmˈpruːvd/).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'I used to procrastinate,' am I talking about the past or the present?" (Expected answer: The past.)</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say 'I have developed better habits,' does it mean I did it in the past and it's still true now?" (Expected answer: Yes, it started in the past and continues to affect the present.)</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Study Habits for Success"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short narrative excerpt from a student's blog post titled "My Journey to Better Focus." The text describes their initial struggles with distraction and procrastination, followed by the strategies they adopted and the positive results they have achieved. The narrative will feature examples of past simple (e.g., "I used to get distracted easily," "I missed assignments") and present perfect for achievements (e.g., "I have learned to manage my time better," "I have seen a great improvement in my grades"). It will also include vocabulary such as <i>study, habits, focus, time, goals, progress, achievements, routines, strategies, productivity, improvement, success</i>.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Comprehension of Written Texts to identify key details and models for personal narrative structure.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> Students read the blog post and answer: "What is the main message the author wants to convey about study habits?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Students match phrases describing "past habits" with "current achievements" from the text. Example: "Used to skip reading" <span style="font-weight: bold;">-></span> "Have developed a frequent reading habit."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>WH-questions:</b> Students identify the answers to questions like, "What strategies has the author implemented to improve focus?" and "How has the author's academic progress changed?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Writing</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Focusing on written accuracy of Present Perfect for achievements, Past Simple for reflection, and appropriate use of vocabulary (<i>manage, procrastinate, improve, develop, reflect, suggest, set goals, practice, focus, achieve, organize, complete, work on, discuss, inspire, evaluate, prioritize, accomplish, effective, frequent, productive, focused, essential, improved, organized, consistent</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Sentence Transformation (Controlled Practice):</b> Students receive a list of sentences describing negative past study habits and rewrite them to show positive changes using the present perfect. Example: "I used to forget my assignments." -> "I have started to organize my assignments in an agenda." The teacher guides students to use different achievement verbs.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Guided Paragraph Writing (Semi-controlled):</b> Students are given sentence starters and a list of target vocabulary words. They write short paragraphs (3-4 sentences) about a specific study habit they have changed. Prompts: "Before, I _________," "Now, I have _________," "This change has helped me _________." (e.g., "Before, I *used to procrastinate* on big projects. Now, I *have developed* an *effective* time *management* plan. This change *has improved* my *productivity* significantly.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Peer Sentence Review (Pair Interaction/Peer Check):</b> Students exchange their guided paragraphs from activity 2 with a partner. Each student uses a simple checklist to review their partner's paragraph for correct use of Past Simple and Present Perfect, vocabulary, and clarity. They provide constructive written feedback focusing on accuracy (e.g., "Check verb tense here," "Good use of 'effective strategy'").</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Study Habits for Success"</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Personal Narrative and Feedback Compilation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Drafting Personal Narratives:</b> Students individually compose a detailed personal narrative (100-150 words) titled "How I Learned to Focus: My Study Habits Journey." The narrative should describe a specific challenge they faced with focus or study habits (using Past Simple), what they *have done* to overcome it (using Present Perfect for achievements), and the *results* they are currently experiencing. They must incorporate at least 8 vocabulary words from the list (e.g., <i>manage, improve, develop, set goals, focus, achieve, organize, consistent, effective, success</i>). (e.g., "A few months ago, I *used to* feel overwhelmed by my *assignments*. I *struggled* to *focus* and my *grades* *were* suffering. Since then, I *have developed* a new *routine*. I *have set* clear *goals* and *prioritize* my *tasks*. This *strategy has improved* my *productivity* and I *have achieved* better *results*.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Peer Feedback Session:</b> In groups of three, students exchange their narratives. Each student reads two peers' narratives and provides constructive written feedback using specific WH-questions as prompts:
                        <ul>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What is one thing your peer *has done* that you find inspiring?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which past habit *did* your peer describe that you can relate to?"</li>
                            <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How *could* your peer *improve* the clarity of their narrative?" (Focus on suggestions for improvement for future writing.)</li>
                        </ul>
                    </li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Compile Feedback:</b> Each student then takes the written feedback they received and compiles it into a short, structured paragraph, paraphrasing suggestions and acknowledgements. This demonstrates the "interactive" part of the specific objective. (e.g., "My peers *have suggested* I elaborate more on my initial struggles. They also noted that my description of how I *have set* new *goals* was very clear and potentially *inspiring*.")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">15 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Peer Assessment Checklist for written narratives and teacher observation of feedback compilation.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their personal narrative and the compiled feedback they received. The teacher observes how students organize and paraphrase the feedback received during the compilation phase.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher & Peer):</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and coherence of the personal narrative (Writing: Can write a detailed narrative).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accurate use of Past Simple and Present Perfect for achievements.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Effective use of at least 8 target vocabulary words.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to compile and paraphrase peer feedback clearly and accurately. (Interactive: Compile feedback).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Overall organization and presentation of the written work.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students answer the following questions on an index card:</p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What *have* I *learned* about writing a personal narrative today?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What *could* I *improve* in my writing for the next assignment?"</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">8 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection Prompt:</b></p>
                <ul>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"How has sharing your personal study journey and receiving feedback helped you *reflect* on your own *progress*?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Which study *strategies* *suggested* by your peers or observed in the model text might you *incorporate* into your own *routines*?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">What worked well in this lesson was the clear progression from controlled practice to free writing and then peer feedback. Students actively used the target grammar (Past Simple and Present Perfect) and vocabulary effectively in their narratives. The collaborative feedback session engaged students in critically evaluating and compiling information, fulfilling both productive and interactive writing objectives. The pronunciation practice of -ed endings and stressing main verbs was integrated naturally during modeling and feedback discussions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">One adjustment needed for next time might be to provide a more explicit rubric for peer feedback to ensure greater consistency in the quality of suggestions. Additionally, providing examples of compiled feedback would help students better structure their responses.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson (Lesson 5 - Mediation & Integration):</b> In the next lesson, students will use their personal narratives and the compiled feedback to contribute to a collaborative "Study Strategies Guide" for younger students, integrating their learned strategies and synthesizing information from multiple sources, thus mediating their understanding to a new audience. This will involve summarizing and presenting information effectively.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="padding: 8px; border: 1px dashed #333;">2 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>